Neighborhoods with the Highest BurdensSouth Bronx / Cross Bronx Corridor (e.g., Hunts Point, Mott Haven, Tremont):Shows prominent dark red highlights across traffic indicators (VMT Total/Trucks), industrial/boiler emissions ($\text{NO}_x$, $\text{PM}_{2.5}$), and acute health outcomes (Asthma ED visits and Respiratory Hospitalizations).High heavy-duty diesel traffic combined with commercial/industrial density creates a compounding air quality burden.Northern & Central Brooklyn (e.g., East New York, Bushwick, Williamsburg):Displays concentrated red strips across primary pollutants ($\text{NO}_2$, $\text{PM}_{2.5}$, Benzene) and high rates of respiratory emergency department visits.Driven by localized boiler emissions, commercial activity, and heavy surface-street transit corridors.High-Density Manhattan Commercial Corridors (e.g., Midtown, Lower East Side/Chinatown, East Harlem):Features intense red bands for mobile sources (VMT Cars/Total), $\text{NO}_2$, Formaldehyde, and Benzene.Building density and stationary heating oil boilers keep localized ambient concentrations elevated despite lower relative proportions of industrial land use.High-Ozone/Downwind Communities (e.g., Southeast Queens, Outer Boroughs):Shows strong red spikes specifically in the Ozone Levels and Ozone Mortality columns.While primary traffic pollutants ($\text{NO}_2$, $\text{PM}_{2.5}$) drop off in these areas, atmospheric secondary reactions produce high ozone levels during warm months, resulting in distinct seasonal health risks.
